  "hints": [
    "The running total is over *days*, so first get one row per day.",
    "A CTE with the daily revenue, then `SUM(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day)`.",
    "Round the revenue and the running total only in the final `SELECT`."
  ],
  "solution": "WITH daily AS (\n  SELECT o.ordered_at AS day, SUM(i.quantity * i.unit_price) AS revenue\n  FROM orders AS o\n  JOIN order_items AS i ON i.order_id = o.id\n  WHERE o.status IN ('paid', 'shipped')\n  GROUP BY o.ordered_at\n)\nSELECT day,\n       ROUND(revenue, 2) AS revenue,\n       ROUND(SUM(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day), 2) AS running_total\nFROM daily\nORDER BY day;\n",
  "explanation": "**The idea.** First aggregate to one row per day (`GROUP BY`), then run a window over those rows: `SUM(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day)` adds up from the first row to the current one. With an `ORDER BY` and no explicit frame, the window is \"from the start up to this row\" (and to the rows that tie with it, which is why each day must be one row first).\n\n**Aggregate, then window.** A window function sees the rows *after* grouping, so the daily totals must come from a subquery or CTE. Mixing them in one `SELECT` (`SUM(SUM(...)) OVER ...`) works in some databases, but is harder to read.\n\n**Rounding.** Round at the end: rounding each day and then adding would drift away from the true running total."
